#import "../math_template.typ" : *

#show : start.with(
  title   : "Gauss' theorem for UFDs",
  author  : "Soham Saha"
)

Given a unique factorization domain $R$, and let us define primitive polynomials in $R[x]$ as all $p(x)=a_0+a_1x+...a_n x^n in R[x]$ such that $gcd_R (a_0,...,a_n)$ is a unit in $R$.

Then, Gauss' lemma states that

1. (_Primitivity statement_) : The set of primitive polynomials in $R[x]$ is closed under multiplication.
2. (_Irreducibility statement_) : Given a #underl[non-constant] polynomial $f(x) in R[x]$, $ f(x) "is irreducible in" R[x]<=>f(x) "is irreducible in" "Frac"(R)[x] and f(x) "is primitive in" R[x]. $

\

Given an infinite strictly increasing chain of principal ideals in $R[x]$, we can get an infinite strictly increasing chain of principal ideals generated by polynomials $p_1,p_2,...$ all of which have the same degree. Suppose $p_i=a_i p_(i+1)$ for some $a_i$ in $R$. None of $a_i$ can be units in $R$. Now, the leading coefficients of $p_1,p_2,...$ generate an ascending chain of principal ideals in $R$ which is not possible as $R$ is UFD. So, $R[x]$ satisfies ACCP.

\

If constant polynomial $p in R[x]$ is irreducible, then it is also irreducible in $R$. Since $R$ is UFD, so $p$ is prime in $R$. So $(p)$ is a prime ideal of $R[x]$. Consider the map $hat(pi):R[x]->(R\/(p))[x]$ defined using $pi:R->R\/(p)$ and $x |-> x$. Since $(p)$ is prime ideal, so $R\/(p)$ is domain, and so is $(R\/(p))[x]$. Suppose $p$ divides $f(x)g(x)$ for some $f(x),g(x) in R[x]$. Then $hat(pi)(f(x)g(x))=hat(pi)(f(x))hat(pi)(g(x))=0$, and since $(R\/(p))[x]$ is integral domain, so assume WLOG that $hat(pi)(f(x))=0$, therefore, $f(x)$ is divisible by $p$  in $R[x]$, showing that $p$ is prime in $R[x]$.

If non-constant polynomial $f(x)$ is irreducible in $R[x]$, then by Gauss lemma, we have that $f(x)$ is irreducible in $"Frac"(R)[x]$ and primitive in $R[x]$. Suppose $f(x)$ divides $g(x)h(x)$ where $g(x),h(x) in R[x]$. Since $"Frac"(R)$ is a field, therefore, $"Frac"(R)[x]$ is an Euclidean domain, and therefore a PID, and therefore an UFD, and therefore all irreducibles are prime in $"Frac"(R)[x]$. So $f(x)$ is prime in $"Frac"(R)[x]$, and WLOG assume that it divides $g(x)$ in $"Frac"(R)[x]$. Since $f(x)$ is primitive in $R[x]$, and it divides $g(x)$ (which belongs to $R[x]$) in $"Frac"(R)[x]$, so it also divides $g(x)$ in $R[x]$. So, $f(x)$ is also prime in $R[x]$.

\

Therefore, in $R[x]$, all irreducibles are prime.

Since $R[x]$ satisfies ACCP and all irreducibles are prime in $R[x]$, therefore, $R[x]$ is a UFD.

#notebox[
  So finally, we get that whenever $R$ is a UFD, so is $R[x]$.
]